WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:03.240
After you've trained a sequence model,

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:09.760
one of the ways you can informally get a sense of what is learned is to have it sample novel sequences.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:12.080
Let's take a look at how you could do that.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:20.960
So remember that a sequence model models the chance of any particular sequence of words as follows.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:28.320
And so what we like to do is sample from this distribution to generate novel sequences of words.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:35.840
So the network was trained using this structure shown at the top.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:39.040
But to sample, you do something slightly different.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:45.760
So what you want to do is first sample what is the first word you want your model to generate.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:51.080
And so for that, you would input the usual x1 equals 0, a0 equals 0.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:58.680
And now your first time step will have some softmax probability over possible outputs.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:05.640
So what you do is you then randomly sample according to this softmax distribution.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:10.920
So what the softmax distribution gives you is it tells you what is the chance of the first word is a,

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.600
what's the chance of the first word is a,

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:16.720
aaron, what's the first chance of the first word is zulu,

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:20.320
what's the chance of the first word is the unknown word token,

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:23.680
maybe what's the chance is a, a, an sentence token.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:32.480
And then you take this vector and use, for example, the NumPy command np.random.choice

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:37.040
to sample according to the distribution defined by this vector probabilities.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.760
And that lets you sample the first words.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:46.640
Next, you then go on to the second time step.

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:53.600
And now, remember that the second time step is expecting this y1 as input.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:58.400
But what you do is you would then take the y1 hat that you just sampled

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:02.640
and pass that in here as the input to the next time step.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:05.280
So whatever word you just chose for the first time step,

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:08.320
pass it as input in the second position.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:13.280
And then this softmax will make a prediction for what is y hat 2.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:16.960
So as a kind of free example, let's say that after you sample the first word,

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:21.760
the first word happened to be z, which is a very common choice of first word.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:33.760
Then you pass in z as x2, which is now equal to y hat 1.

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:38.160
And now you're trying to figure out what is the chance of whatever the second word is,

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:40.400
given that the first word is z.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:43.360
And this is going to be y hat 2.

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:49.120
Then you again use this type of sampling function to sample y hat 2.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:52.960
And then at the next time step, you take whatever choice you had,

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:59.920
represented, say, as a one-hot encoding, and pass that to the next time step.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:04.480
And then you sample the third word, take that, whatever you chose,

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:08.880
and you keep going until you get to the last time step.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:11.840
And so how do you know when the sequence ends?

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:18.080
Well, one thing you could do is if the end-of-sentence token is part of your vocabulary,

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:21.840
you could keep sampling until you generate the EOS token.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:24.880
And that tells you you've hit the end of a sentence, and then you can stop.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:28.480
Or alternatively, if you did not include this in your vocabulary,

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:33.040
then you can also just decide to sample 20 words or 100 words or something,

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:36.560
and then keep going until you've reached that number of time steps.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:43.120
And this physical procedure will sometimes generate a unknown word token.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:47.520
If you want to make sure that your algorithm never generates this token,

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:53.680
one thing you could do is just reject any sample that came out as unknown word token

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:57.200
and just keep resampling for the rest of the vocabulary until you get a word

00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:58.400
that's not an unknown word.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:01.120
Or you could just leave it in the output as well,

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:04.000
if you don't mind having an unknown word output.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:08.480
But so this is how you would generate a randomly chosen sentence

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:11.200
from your RNN language model.

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:15.040
Now, so far, we've been building a word-level RNN,

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:19.760
by which I mean the vocabulary are words from English.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:22.480
Depending on your application, one thing you could do

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:26.080
is also build a character-level RNN.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:32.320
So in this case, your vocabulary would be just the alphabets up to Z,

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:37.120
and as well as maybe space punctuation, if you wish,

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:40.800
the digits 0 to 9.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:44.240
And if you want to distinguish the uppercase and lowercase,

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:47.520
you can include the uppercase alphabets as well.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.640
And one thing you could do is actually just look at your training set,

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:52.480
look at your training set corpus,

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:54.720
and look at the characters that appear there

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:57.120
and use that to define your vocabulary.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:00.640
And if you build a character-level language model,

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:03.360
rather than a word-level language model,

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:08.240
then your sequence, Y1, Y2, Y3,

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:12.400
would be the individual characters in your training data,

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:15.360
rather than the individual words in your training data.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:17.520
So for our previous example,

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:22.160
the sentence cats average 15 hours of sleep a day.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:29.360
In this example, C would be Y1, A would be Y2, T would be Y3.

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:32.880
The space would be Y4, and so on.

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:36.160
Using a character-level language model has some pros and cons.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:40.640
One is that you don't ever have to worry about unknown word tokens.

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:46.240
In particular, a character-level language model is able to assign a sequence

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:48.560
like MAO, a non-zero probability,

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:53.280
whereas if MAO was not in your vocabulary for the word-level language model,

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:56.400
you just have to assign it the unknown word token.

00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:00.480
But the main disadvantage of the character-level language model

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:04.560
is that you end up with much longer sequences.

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:08.160
So many English sentences will have 10 to 20 words,

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:11.600
but may have many, many dozens of characters.

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:16.320
And so character-language models are not as good as word-level language models

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:18.800
at capturing long-range dependencies

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:21.200
between how the earlier parts of the sentence

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:23.520
also affect the later part of the sentence.

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:28.640
And character-language models are also just more computationally expensive to train.

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:31.360
So the trend I've been seeing in natural language processing

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:36.160
is that for the most part, word-level language models are still used,

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:38.720
but as computers get faster,

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:41.520
there are more and more applications where people are,

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:43.680
you know, at least in some special cases,

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:47.360
starting to look at more character-level models.

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:49.120
But they do tend to be much harder

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:51.280
and much more computationally expensive to train,

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:53.280
so they're not in widespread use today.

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:55.520
Except for maybe specialized applications

00:06:55.520 --> 00:07:00.080
where you might need to deal with unknown words or other vocabulary words a lot.

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:04.000
Or they're also used in more specialized applications

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:06.480
where you have a more specialized vocabulary.

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:08.800
So armed with these methods,

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:13.520
what you can now do is build an RNN to look at the corpus of English text,

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:16.160
build a word-level, build a character-language model,

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:19.840
or build a word-level, or build a character-level language model,

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:23.360
and then sample from the language model that you've trained.

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:26.160
So here are some fun examples of texts

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:28.160
that were sampled from a language model,

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:30.720
actually from a character-level language model.

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:34.000
You get to implement something like this yourself in the programming exercise.

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:36.720
If the model was trained on news articles,

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:39.120
then it generates text like that shown on the left.

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:42.320
And you know, this looks vaguely like news texts,

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:44.320
not quite grammatical,

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:48.480
but maybe sounds a little bit like things that could be appearing in news,

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:50.320
concussion epidemic to be examined.

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:52.160
And it was trained on Shakespearean texts,

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:55.600
and it generates stuff that, you know, sounds like Shakespeare could have written it.

00:07:55.600 --> 00:07:57.440
The mortal moon hath an eclipse in love,

00:07:57.440 --> 00:07:59.920
and subject to this doubt art in other disfills.

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:02.080
When best to be my love to me sees have,

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:04.720
but whose are rules of mine are his?

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:08.880
So that's it for the basic RNN

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:11.360
and how you can build a language model using it,

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:14.960
as well as sample from the language model that you've trained.

